EXT. SNOWFIELD - BELOW A CRASHED BUSH PLANE - DAY

White. Everything white. A twisted aluminum wing juts from a drift like a broken bone. Wind scours the ridge.

ASTRID VESTERGAARD-NNAMDI, 30s, in a torn orange parka, kneels beside a battered satchel. Her face is cracked with cold. A tiny emergency radio sits in her lap, its antenna taped to a ski pole.

She lifts it. Presses the key. Releases.

Static.

She tries again. Static.

She sets it down. Breathes. Fumbles in the satchel, withdraws a small digital recorder, and presses PLAY.

ASTRID (V.O.)
(recording, scratchy)
Day two. I'm okay. Cold, but okay. I keep calling. Nobody answers.

She listens, eyes shut.

ASTRID (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recording)
If you find this, tell Mom I wasn't scared.

Her mouth twists. She turns the recorder off.

She looks at the wing. At the sky. At the ridge, where a shape, grey, hunched, sways.

She stills. Her hand finds the pistol flare in her pocket.

The shape resolves. A caribou. It stares. She stares. A moment of animal truce.

It turns and ambles over the ridge.

She lets out a shuddering breath, laughs once, short, ragged.

The radio crackles.

Three clicks. A pause. Three clicks again, from far away.

She grabs it. Fumbles. Presses the key.

ASTRID
(hoarse)
This is Astrid. This is Astrid Vestergaard. I'm alive.

She drops her head. Snow stings her cheek.

She closes her eyes and listens to the radio.
